"""Expand one existing wildfire acquisition note; preserve homepage and library."""
import json,re
import expand_hold_period as publisher
SLUG='wildfire-insurability-str'
TITLE='How Do You Check Wildfire Insurability Before Buying an STR | BNB Accelerator'
H1='How Do You Check Wildfire Insurability Before Buying an STR?'
DESC='Before buying an STR, verify wildfire coverage and mitigation conditions. Use a funded-work register and labeled purchase-cash and renewal stress cases.'
FAQ=[
 ('Does the seller\'s wildfire policy establish the buyer\'s coverage?','No. Obtain an adviser-reviewed proposal for the buyer, address, actual rental use and intended dates. Identify outstanding underwriting conditions and the evidence needed for coverage to become effective.'),
 ('Does mitigation guarantee an insurer will bind or renew?','No. Document the actual work, completion evidence and insurer response. Risk reduction, current underwriting acceptance and future renewal are separate decisions; no discount or renewal is assumed.'),
 ('Is a FAIR plan automatically complete STR insurance?','No. Availability, eligibility and terms vary. Have a licensed adviser review the actual property, rental use and all needed coverage, including any separate liability or income protection.'),
 ('Can an evacuation order be treated as guaranteed insured income?','No. Ask the adviser to review the actual business-income and civil-authority provisions, triggers, limitations and exclusions. Fund a no-proceeds scenario rather than assuming every closure is reimbursed.')
]
BODY='''<p class="lead">Before buying an STR exposed to wildfire, request insurance terms for the actual address, purchaser and rental use, then identify the work and evidence required before coverage can become effective. A seller's premium, a mitigation certificate or a preliminary quote cannot establish all three. The acquisition decision is whether the supported coverage, remaining conditions, dated cash and ongoing cost fit the buyer's plan before relevant contract protections expire.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the wildfire coverage and funded-work register to an STR acquisition call</a>. BNB Accelerator can integrate verified costs and unresolved conditions within its acquisition agreement; licensed insurance professionals, local officials and counsel make their own coverage, safety and legal determinations.</p>
<h2>Review coverage for the buyer's use, not the seller's bill</h2>
<p>Give a licensed adviser the intended ownership and insured parties, guest stays, capacity, amenities, management arrangement, renovation or vacancy period, settlement date and proposed first arrival. Request the actual proposed forms, endorsements, limits, deductibles and conditions, not only a premium total. Ask which answers remain subject to inspection or insurer approval and what evidence the lender requires.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://content.naic.org/article/protecting-your-home-wildfires-steps-you-can-take">NAIC's September 14, 2023 wildfire guidance</a> recommends checking actual wildfire coverage and any separate deductible with an insurance professional. Its household guidance also describes risk-reduction work involving vegetation, debris and building materials. It does not certify this rental's policy, prescribe the correct site-specific work or promise underwriting acceptance.</p>
<p>Review building and contents protection separately from liability and income protection. Ask how rebuilding limits, valuations, deductibles and relevant exclusions operate under the proposed wording. Do not infer that an LLC selects a particular product or that a personal policy covers paying guests. The <a href="/blog/str-insurance-quote-before-purchase-offer/">pre-offer insurance guide</a> covers the general quote, contract-deadline and premium workflow; this register focuses on wildfire-related conditions and how required work is funded.</p>
<h2>Give each mitigation condition an owner and a completion test</h2>
<p>Ask the adviser to identify requirements actually imposed for the proposed policy. Request an appropriate qualified assessment of roof, vegetation, siding, vents, water and access where relevant, without treating this list as a complete safety inspection. Distinguish a recommendation, a local legal requirement, and an insurer's underwriting condition. One document may not satisfy the other reviewers.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Open item</th><th>Evidence and responsible reviewer</th><th>Purchase-cash consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Actual wildfire terms</td><td>Adviser-reviewed forms, insured parties, rental use, limits and deductibles</td><td>Compare the accepted coverage and owner exposure, not premiums alone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Required physical work</td><td>Written condition, qualified scope, necessary permissions and contractor pricing</td><td>Replace existing budget lines before adding distinct new work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completion before binding</td><td>Who verifies work, which photos or inspection are needed, and insurer response</td><td>Identify cash and timing before the coverage effective date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interruption or restricted access</td><td>Actual income/civil-authority provisions and local official guidance</td><td>Fund a no-proceeds case; insurance review is not evacuation planning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Future review</td><td>Renewal dates, changing operations and maintenance responsibilities</td><td>Keep annual cost sensitivity separate from upfront obligations</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Record the amount, payer, due date, contractor availability, required approval and unresolved response beside each row. A seller credit does not complete work or establish that insurance will be effective on time. Counsel and the settlement team should evaluate any agreement for seller-completed work, access before closing, credits, extensions or other protection; do not undertake work on someone else's property without authority.</p>
<p>Do not count a distant track as an alternate exit because coverage is obtainable. The <a href="/blog/wildfire-evacuation-route-before-buying-str/">separate evacuation-route acquisition guide</a> addresses legal and physical access, official alerts and guest response. A coverage decision cannot certify a safe exit or override current emergency directions.</p>
<h2>Evaluate a backup market without inventing complete coverage</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/fair-access-to-insurance-requirements-plans">NAIC's FAIR-plan explanation, updated December 13, 2024</a>, describes residual property markets and basic protection for properties difficult to insure in the regular market. It also emphasizes variation and limitations. This background is not proof of a current STR's eligibility, complete coverage, an available limit or an acceptable premium in any state.</p>
<p>If a backup proposal is considered, ask the adviser what it actually covers for this owner and use, which needs remain unresolved, and whether additional policies are required and obtainable. Have the lender review acceptability. Price the complete supported arrangement once, including actual deposits or other dated charges; do not assume the first quoted property policy also supplies liability and income protection. This page makes no nationwide claim about state-plan availability, notices, discounts or nonrenewal rights.</p>
<h2>Original funded-work example: replace an overlapping cost</h2>
<p>Invented arithmetic only, not typical prices or a client transaction. Assume $300,000 is available and the initial allocations are $210,000 for down payment and closing, $32,000 for setup and $40,000 retained reserve: $282,000 total and $18,000 unallocated. All baseline insurance payments are already included. The $32,000 setup line already contains $6,000 for roof work.</p>
<p>Assume the actual required roof scope costs $14,000, separate vegetation work is $4,000, and a distinct completion review is $1,000. Replace the roof allowance: the incremental work is $14,000 − $6,000 + $4,000 + $1,000 = $13,000. Revised setup is $45,000, and total allocation becomes $295,000, leaving $5,000 while keeping the $40,000 reserve intact. Adding the entire $14,000 again would duplicate the existing $6,000.</p>
<p>If a completion delay adds another $6,000 of genuinely additional net obligations, not already included in closing, setup or reserve allocations, the total becomes $301,000: a $1,000 gap against available funds. Replace that assumed delay amount with dated actual bills and identify which receipts disappear or expenses are avoided. Do not call forgone gross bookings another invoice or add costs already in the base case a second time.</p>
<p>The buyer needs a supported way to resolve the gap or alter the transaction. Spending the retained reserve changes the reserve assumption rather than making the gap disappear. A possible refund, future claim or high salary is not cash already available for pre-binding work. These allocations do not compute investment return or the appropriate reserve for this property.</p>
<h2>Stress renewal cost without predicting a nonrenewal</h2>
<p>Keep a second, annual worksheet. Suppose an independently supported operating case has $9,500 annual cash after its current insurance expense. An invented $3,000 incremental renewal-cost sensitivity leaves $6,500 before other changes. That is a stress input, not a quoted future price, expected percentage increase or prediction that the policy will renew. It must not be added again to today's cash-to-close unless an actual distinct payment is due then.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/business-interruption-and-business-owner-policy">NAIC business-interruption overview, updated June 25, 2026</a>, distinguishes property damage and income protection and discusses conditional civil-authority coverage. Have the adviser explain the actual triggers, waiting periods, duration, exclusions and access-related provisions. Smoke concerns, reduced demand or an evacuation order do not automatically establish an insured income payment under every contract. The worksheet assumes no claim proceeds; it is not a settlement calculation.</p>
<h2>Resolve the conditions before committing the remaining capital</h2>
<p>Proceed when intended use and actual terms have been reviewed, required work and completion evidence have a credible authorized path, lender conditions are resolved, and dated payments and the operating downside fit the buyer's standards. Reprice a still-viable property when verified costs change its offer ceiling. Seek a valid extension or decline when a decisive condition remains unanswered. Counsel determines actual contractual rights, not this article.</p>
<p>Assign ongoing mitigation maintenance and future insurance review to a named responsible party. Keep policy documents, claims histories and financial records in agreed secure channels. <a href="/apply/">Discuss the acquisition shortlist with the completed register</a>, and use the <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> for funding and diligence beyond this hazard.</p>
<p class="small">Primary NAIC materials reviewed October 7, 2026; source dates are September 2023, December 2024 and June 2026, not newly issued policy terms. Educational only, not insurance, fire-safety, claims, legal, lending, tax or personalized investment advice. All numerical examples are hypothetical. No eligibility, binding, renewal, coverage, payment, refund or investment result is guaranteed.</p>'''
if __name__=='__main__':
 si=publisher.ROOT/'sitemap/index.html';old=si.read_text();label=re.search(r'(<a href="/blog/'+SLUG+r'/">)[^<]+',old)[0]
 publisher.SLUG,publisher.TITLE,publisher.H1=SLUG,TITLE,H1
 publisher.DESC,publisher.BODY,publisher.FAQ=DESC,BODY,FAQ
 publisher.main('2026-09-23','data-wildfire-insurability-review-link','Before committing capital to required work, use the <a href="/blog/wildfire-insurability-str/">wildfire coverage and funded-mitigation register</a>. Binding conditions, annual renewal sensitivity and evacuation planning remain separate.')
 si.write_text(re.sub(r'(<a href="/blog/'+SLUG+r'/">)[^<]+',lambda m:label,si.read_text()))
 p=publisher.ROOT/'blog'/SLUG/'index.html';s=p.read_text();assert 'FAQPage' not in s
 node={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage','mainEntity':[{'@type':'Question','name':q,'acceptedAnswer':{'@type':'Answer','text':a}} for q,a in FAQ]}
 s=s.replace('</head>','<script type="application/ld+json">'+json.dumps(node,indent=2)+'</script>\n</head>',1)
 s=s.replace('<span>Updated October 6, 2026</span>','<span>Updated October 7, 2026</span>',1).replace('property="og:type" content="website"','property="og:type" content="article"',1)
 p.write_text(s)
